:0.012733464780521266,"_gp_fcf_yield":false,"_score_fcf_yield":0.0,"_gate_int_coverage":1.9368238857637385,"_gp_int_coverage":false,"_score_int_coverage":4.9,"_gate_net_debt_ebitda":4.5327807884390605,"_gp_net_debt_ebitda":false,"_score_net_debt_ebitda":0.0,"_gate_accruals":-0.03548938532793711,"_gp_accruals":true,"_score_accruals":31.7,"_gate_rev_volatility":0.07649965610244538,"_gp_rev_volatility":true,"_score_rev_volatility":92.4,"_gate_margin_vs_hist":-0.06665860864473037,"_gp_margin_vs_hist":true,"_score_margin_vs_hist":100.0,"_gate_piotroski":3,"_gp_piotroski":false,"_score_piotroski":33.3,"_gate_spread":0.107474233322875,"_gp_spread":true,"_score_spread":53.7,"_gate_roic_consistency":1.2445778765639404,"_gp_roic_consistency":false,"_score_roic_consistency":0.0,"_gate_incr_roic":-0.007316711761002853,"_gp_incr_roic":false,"_score_incr_roic":14.2,"_gate_margin_advantage":-0.000537309621650231,"_gp_margin_advantage":false,"_score_margin_advantage":33.2,"_gate_pool_share":-0.05639936659078815,"_gp_pool_share":false,"_score_pool_share":17.4,"_gate_rev_durability":0.10837681594079496,"_gp_rev_durability":true,"_score_rev_durability":79.2,"_gate_fcf_durability":-0.21165514786877193,"_gp_fcf_durability":false,"_score_fcf_durability":0.0,"_gate_margins":0.002935709375838419,"_gp_margins":true,"_score_margins":52.9,"_gate_fund_growth":0,"_gp_fund_growth":false,"_score_fund_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.0,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":false,"_score_shrhldr_yield":11.1,"_gate_share_shrink":0.05533431170002845,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.007858058645932421,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":86.9,"_gate_insider_own":0.00417,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":2.8,"_gate_insider_buying":0.0,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":0.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"James Hardie Industries plc engages in the manufacture and sale of fiber cement, fiber gypsum, and cement bonded boards in the United States, Australia, Europe, and New Zealand. It operates through four segments: Siding & Trim, Deck, Rail & Accessories, Australia & New Zealand, and Europe. The Siding & Trim segment manufactures fiber cement and PVC siding and trim products, mouldings, interior linings, and accessories under brands such as Hardie, AZEK Exteriors, and Versatex are used in both residential new construction and repair and remodel applications. The Deck, Rail & Accessories segment produces decking, railing, cladding, pergolas, cabanas, and related accessories, including wood-alternative decking and railing solutions offered under the TimberTech, ULTRALOX, and INTEX brands, as well as functional and decorative outdoor accessories, including drink rails, structural mounting posts, lighting systems and gate kits; and adjustable louvered pergola systems through StruXure. The Australia & New Zealand segment manufactures and sells fiber cement products for residential and commercial applications, including exterior cladding, interior wall linings, flooring, eaves and soffits, and fa\u00e7ade systems, primarily under the Hardie brand. The Europe segment produces fiber gypsum products and cement-bonded boards under the fermacell brand, and fiber cement products under the Hardie brand, serving residential and commercial repair, remodel, and new construction markets. The company offers its products through a network of specialty building products distributors, lumberyards, and home improvement retailers to builder's merchants, remodelers, DIY stores, and professional contractors. James Hardie Industries plc was founded in 1888 and is headquartered in Dublin, Ireland.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Aaron M. Erter | CEO & Executive Director | Age 51 | Compensation: $1.9M (FY2026)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 7K people. Revenue per employee of $645K ranks in the above the sector median among Basic Materials peers, with revenue growing 9%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $27K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $5K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 3\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (6/10).","financial_summary":["James Hardie Industries plc. is a large-cap basic materials business with a $16.2B market cap on $4.8B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +9% over the past three years and +11% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 20% against a 9% cost of capital \u2014 a 11-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat, with gross margins sitting at 35%.","The business throws off $206M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 4 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 5.67\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 4.5\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 1.9\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 162% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":1.6193560405444332},{"text":"Piotroski score 3/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $29.0M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Carries more leverage than most Basic Materials peers \u2014 a smaller cushion for the inevitable bad year","sev":"amber","cat":"balance_sheet"},{"text":"Weakness in commodity prices pressures materials companies","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Trade policy and tariff whiplash \u2014 materials producers are first-order casualties of protectionist swings, and margin gets squeezed from both input and output pricing.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["ROIC sits in the top quartile of Basic Materials peers \u2014 the moat is wider than the neighbourhood"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"James Hardie (ASX:JHX) Shares May Be 19% Undervalued Following Index Inclusion","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/au/materials/asx-jhx/james-hardie-industries-shares/news/james-hardie-asxjhx-shares-may-be-19-undervalued-following-i","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794573108.989,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.962,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Why Everyone Is Watching James Hardie Industries (ASX:JHX) Right Now","source":"finance.yahoo.com","link":"https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/why-everyone-watching-james-hardie-111211422.html","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794568331.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":-0.178,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"James Hardie (ASX:JHX) North American Remodelling Demand in Focus","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/au/stocks/midcap/james-hardie-asxjhx-north-american-remodelling-demand-in-focus","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794552600.0,"ori